      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the physics explanation.\n\nIn a DC circuit, capacitors don't react to the AC voltage, so they don't affect the circuit's impedance. However, in an AC circuit, the capacitor's reactance (Xc) is a measure of its opposition to the changing current.\n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance is Xc = 1 / (2πfC), where:\n\n- Xc is the capacitive reactance (measured in ohms, Ω)\n- f is the frequency of the AC voltage (measured in hertz, Hz)\n- C is the capacitance (measured in farads, F)\n\nTo calculate Xc, simply plug in the values for f and C, and rearrange the formula to solve for Xc.\n\nFor example, if you have a capacitor with a capacitance of 100 μF (0.0001 F) and you want to calculate its reactance at a frequency of 60 Hz, you would use the formula:\n\nXc = 1 / (2π × 60 Hz × 0.0001 F)\nXc = 1 / (2π × 0",
